{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_konsfexvnmrhbmxlh3prxk6expjqd4eulqdd6phiirl4xxetbana","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete — **the Rust translation is byte-identical to the C for every input tested. No behavioural fixes to `src/lib.rs` were needed.**\n\n## What the library is\nThree exported functions in `c_src/src/lib.c`: `cleanup(int,int,int,int)`, `print_result(const char*,int)`, `cleanup_resources(char*)`. No enums, asserts, `#ifdef`s, runtime options, or executable target.\n\n## Phase A artifacts (crate root)\n- **`SYMBOLS.md`** — 3 C exports, all present in the Rust `.so`; symbol diff empty, 0 unresolved non-libc imports.\n- **`ERRORS.md`** — 7 rejection rows + 5 generic FFI boundaries, all ✅.\n- **`CONFIGS.md`** — 22 configuration rows across the 8 axes the C actually branches on, all `[x]`.\n\n## Phases B–D\n33 differential tests (`tests/phase_b_configs.rs`, `tests/phase_c_errors.rs`), all loading **both** `.so`s via `libloading` and comparing return values *and* captured `printf` bytes on fd 1. Coverage includes the 5⁴ value-class cross-product, the 6⁴ overflow cross-product, ~11k randomized fixed-seed inputs, NULL/empty/64 KiB/format-string/non-UTF-8 labels, and the composed 3-function pipeline. `verify.sh` runs symbol parity plus all 6 feature/profile combinations: **ALL VERIFICATION CHECKS PASSED**.\n\n## Three real problems I found and fixed — all in the harness, not the translation\n1. **Stale-artifact blindness (most important).** `cargo test` never builds the `cdylib`, so the harness silently loaded a leftover `libcleanup_lib.so` from an earlier `cargo build`. I caught this with a deliberate mutation — removing the `case 10:` fall-through — which the whole suite *still passed*. The harness now builds the cdylib itself and asserts the `.so` is newer than `src/lib.rs`. Re-running the same mutation, plus a second one to the output format string, now fails 5 and 14 tests respectively, so the suite is demonstrably non-vacuous.\n2. **`static` inside a generic fn.** My fd-1 capture mutex was declared inside `capture_stdout<F>`, so it was monomorphised per closure type — every caller got its own mutex and there was no mutual exclusion at all. Hoisted to module scope.\n3. **libtest progress text leaking into captures.** Concurrent tests write `\"test foo ... ok\"` to the same fd 1, corrupting comparisons non-deterministically. Fixed by forcing `RUST_TEST_THREADS=1` via `.cargo/config.toml` and flushing both the Rust and libc stdout buffers before redirecting.\n\nTwo notes on judgement calls, since they are the places where \"passing\" is weaker than it looks:\n- **`ERRORS.md` row 2 (`malloc` failure)** cannot be forced through the FFI boundary. I did not fake it; I assert the observable contract instead — both libraries take the same branch, and the C's error path returns the *accumulated* `result` rather than a sentinel, which the test pins down. Genuinely testing it would need allocator interposition.\n- **Double-free / free-of-stack-pointer on `cleanup_resources`** is UB in C and would abort both libraries non-deterministically, so it is documented as out-of-contract rather than tested.\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: `cargo` had no network access, so I used the locally cached `libloading 0.8.9` with `--offline`. `Cargo.toml` requests `libloading = \"0.8\"` as instructed, but a clean checkout on a networked machine will need to resolve it normally."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":409,"cache_read_input_tokens":86836,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":409,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d3d058ac-6c74-4dce-9790-b3cddc839c96","uuid":"185329e0-be15-4a3f-bf1b-c9a3c181e29b","timestamp":"2026-09-05T13:45:48.184Z"}
